{"source": "accepted-run", "id": 33, "type": "review_finished", "review": {"scores": {"looks_like_the_thing": 4, "detail": 4, "dimensions": 3, "proportion": 4, "print_intent": 4}, "total": 19, "band": "adopt-ready", "pass": true, "tells": ["Paired radii: inner_radius equals corner_radius (both 4) and outer_radius equals gusset_edge_radius (both 1). Each has a stated role, so this is a near-miss rather than a default-everything fillet, but the coincidence is worth one sentence in the docstring.", "gusset_thickness 5 and gusset_reach 0.7 are chosen round numbers with no reason given in the brief or source (perimeter count, screw clearance, or stiffness target). Not a default, but not justified either."], "fixes": [{"rank": 1, "text": "State why gusset_thickness is 5 and gusset_reach is 0.7. A 5 mm rib is about 12 perimeters at 0.4 mm and the ramp foot at x=58 clears the shelf screws at x=44 by 14 mm; write that down or change the values to ones you can defend. No geometry change needed."}, {"rank": 2, "text": "The wall holes sit at z=44, the centre of the free leg, because the brief's assumption says 'centred'. For pull-out the moment arm from the bottom edge would be longer with the pair nearer the top. Brief wins today; flag it for the next brief revision rather than changing the design."}, {"rank": 3, "text": "The shelf counterbore floors are two 8 mm bridged rings at z=4.2 (the 68.7 mm2 of flat underside in the report). This is acknowledged and acceptable, but confirm on the first print that the ring did not sag into the counterbore, since a sagged floor lifts the screw head."}, {"rank": 4, "text": "If a load test shows the wall leg delaminating across layers, print on a side face (y=-30 down) instead. That aligns both legs and the rib with the load and keeps all four counterbores as teardrops. Only do this if the test fails; the brief pins shelf-face-down."}], "good": "Thickness is derived from the M4 counterbore depth plus a floor rather than guessed, and the print orientation is carried all the way through: teardrop wall counterbores, a self-supporting chamfer instead of a fillet on the bed corner, sharp bed and wall faces, and the rib sunk half a plate so the u"}}
